  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nScattered showers and storms will continue this \nafternoon, mainly over the Coastal Plains. Surface obs and TPW \nsatellite imagery show a healthy plume of moisture (PWAT around 2.1-\n2.3 inches) over the coastal waters and counties. This is mainly \ndriven by a weak surface trough/boundary pushing along the \nmiddle/upper TX coast this afternoon. The combination of this \ntrough, daytime heating and seabreeze interaction will keep \nrain/storm chances going through early this evening, though some \nlingering showers may persist offshore as the weak trough moves over \nthe region before weakening later tonight. Brief heavy downpours, \nfrequent lightning and gusty winds are possible with any strong \ncells mainly this afternoon. \n\nA drier pattern quickly returns to the region Friday into the \nweekend. A strong mid to upper level ridge centered over the \nsoutheastern CONUS will continue to move westward and expand over \nthe region over the next couple of days. Onshore (easterly surface \nwinds) will prevail at the surface, bringing a warm and more humid \nGulf air mass into the region. Therefore, while subsidence aloft \nremains strong, we cannot rule out isolated convection, particularly \nin the afternoon with the combination of peak daytime heating and \nsea/baybreeze interaction. Overall, slight rain/storm chances remain \nin the forecast Friday through the weekend. Rain/storm chances will \ncontinue with a daily chance (15 to 20%) Monday through the \nremainder of the week. \n\nWill continue to monitor beach conditions over the next few days. A \nlong easterly fetch will develop across the northwestern Gulf over \nthe next few days. This could result in possible coastal flooding, \nespecially during times of high tides and an increased risk of rip \ncurrents.  \n\nJM",
